{
	title: 'Sessions name themselves',
	spec: `Sessions keep a neutral Session <id> placeholder until the conversation model supplies a human-readable title through the existing command tool with /rename <title>. Never assign a first-message excerpt, including in backfill. Explicit spawn names remain until renamed. Naming reminders accompany substantive user turns 1, 2, 3 and thereafter every fourth (7, 11, ...), as append-only hidden provider context, with durable crash-reconciled counters. Ask for a specific 3–7-word description, at most 60 Unicode characters, in the user language, only when the current title is a placeholder or the main task has drifted; never copy a request or polish wording needlessly. Never instruct XML rename suffixes. Subsequent substantive prompts include the current title so old reminders cannot obscure later renames. No separate naming model call, agent turn or spawn slot.
Human and model renames share the existing command tool and /rename history path, available on every turn. Manual names do not lock automatic naming; latest successful rename wins. Validate the entire title before changing state, rejecting controls, terminal escapes, nested markup, empty or overlong text; normalize ordinary whitespace. Broadcast and persist successful changes. /rename with no argument (or auto) clears to a placeholder and resets scheduling. The legacy backfill command explains that naming happens in conversation and never manufactures excerpt titles. Successful command output contains /rename, previous and new names; command history preserves both human and model calls for a separate Ctrl-F follow-up.
Legacy trailing XML rename suffixes no longer act, but stay hidden in terminal and web assistant-text display regardless of naming eligibility flags, including split streaming and either summary suffix order. Original provider replay and signed history remain immutable. Literal examples in prose, quotations and fenced code remain visible. Legacy naming metadata/history fields stay readable but ownership/version confer no rename permissions. Scheduling excludes slash commands, inbox prompts, tool rounds and assistant continuations; restart/reconnect must not duplicate counters. Tests cover placeholders, model command renaming, human-title drift, scheduling/restart, validation, broadcasts and leaked legacy controls, not exact model wording.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Research 2026-09-30: Codex TUI at openai/codex 2a34aef79484bf769b4b225f3f595755f2962170 auto-generates while unnamed on a completed substantive user-message event, in a hidden temporary thread. It prefers gpt-5.6-luna at low effort only for ChatGPT accounts where that model is listed; otherwise current model. Structured title <=36 characters, under five words where possible, imperative verb, user language; input bounded to 960 bytes. Manual rename cancels pending generation. Later rename suggestions use eight recent substantive messages, not periodic autonomous re-titling. Sources: https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/2a34aef79484bf769b4b225f3f595755f2962170/codex-rs/tui/src/app/thread_title.rs and codex-rs/tui/src/app/thread_routing.rs.',
		'Claude Code docs: first-prompt title is a background small/fast request (normally Haiku); interactive unnamed sessions also get cwd+suffix default display names. Accepting a plan replaces an AI-generated title, but not an explicit name. Direct shell -p runs do not auto-title. Public docs do not disclose an exact title prompt/character cap, so do not invent one. Source: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sessions .',
		'Pi at badlogic/pi-mono 1b347794e2a630e4359f2584f4eea388145d0ddf documents explicit /name, --name and extension setSessionName, not a built-in automatic LLM naming policy. Source: https://github.com/badlogic/pi-mono/blob/1b347794e2a630e4359f2584f4eea388145d0ddf/packages/coding-agent/docs/sessions.md and docs/session-format.md; examples/extensions/session-name.ts is a manual command demonstration, not an auto-title feature.',
		'OpenCode at anomalyco/opencode f66b86ceec1a497417f750b88a06cf6923c5c75f: top-level default-title sessions with exactly one non-synthetic user message invoke a hidden title agent, no tools, two retries; agent model override > configured small_model > provider small model > current model. Prompt asks single-line <=50 characters, user language/main topic, exact technical terms; application takes first nonempty line after removing think tags and caps at 100 characters. No periodic re-titling in ensureTitle. Sources: https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/f66b86ceec1a497417f750b88a06cf6923c5c75f/packages/opencode/src/session/prompt.ts ; packages/opencode/src/agent/prompt/title.txt ; packages/opencode/src/provider/provider.ts. Hidden small calls in other harnesses are a useful precedent, not a reason to replay a full Hal context in another model.',
		'Naming nudges are stored atomically on the accepted user history record (naming: turn/version/name/eligible), and replay appends its hidden meta text. The metadata counter reconciles from that immutable record after a crash; the same ownership epoch avoids duplicates on resume. Eligible turns are 1, 2, 3, 7, 11, etc. Controls occupy their own trailing line; a trailing summary may precede or follow them. Display-only naming flags travel with text blocks/events; provider replay retains original control text and omits that flag. Legacy missing/blank/id/Session-id labels are automatic, all other absent-ownership names are manual. Backfill streams histories one candidate at a time and yields between metadata entries; repeat /rename backfill cancels. No automatic startup disk-wide scan.',
		'Backfill loads readline only when explicitly invoked, keeping its machinery off the host startup import path. History validates the persisted naming scheduling fields. Verification: functional tests pass (1,271 cases), TypeScript and lint pass; startup.test timing budget fails on the loaded host (also when invoked alone: 237 ms UI versus 150 ms), not a functional failure. Existing unrelated fixtures explicitly select manual naming so their exact conversation contracts remain independent of title nudges.',
		'The command-based redesign supersedes the original implementation notes about prompt excerpts, manual ownership locks, XML actions, naming flags and background backfill. Legacy schema fields remain readable; version now only separates counter reset epochs. Both clients hide old suffixes without granting them action semantics. Successful /rename output retains old and new titles; task c8 covers current-session Ctrl-F discovery.',
		'Verification of the command-based redesign: 31 focused naming, command, replay-context and web tests pass; the terminal legacy-suffix regression also passes. Source-only TypeScript and lint pass. Full-suite validation was interrupted by a host restart after terminal end-to-end timeouts; it is not claimed green.',
		'Direct history fixtures isolate naming.prepare just as shared host fixtures do, instead of relying on manual name ownership to suppress unrelated reminder metadata. The web transport regression expects the persisted naming-counter meta broadcast; both transports receive identical events.',
		'Latest broad functional verification (excluding terminal e2e and startup timing checks): 1301 pass, 1 skip, 3 fail. The failures are a status-label expectation polluted by cached model data and two spawn failures from suite-global tab state pointing at a deleted fixture directory; the two spawn checks pass individually. Naming, rename-command, history, web transport and legacy display regressions pass individually. Source-only typecheck and lint pass. Do not claim the entire suite green.',
		'Nudges go only to interactive tabs (not autoclose: hal -p, subagents, benchmarks) and only while 4 or more tabs are open (user decision 8 Oct): naming matters when the user juggles tabs.'
